// Woolly Worm: the old palmered caterpillar of Missouri, made famous by Don Martinez; the Woolly Bugger's parent.

id woolly-worm
name "Woolly Worm"
aliases "Wooly Worm"
type nymph
imitates stonefly attractor
naturals stonefly-nymph damselfly-nymph caddis-larva-free
difficulty beginner
species trout bass panfish
season march april may june july august september october
origin region="Missouri, USA"
description "A short tuft of red wool, a black chenille body and a grizzly hackle palmered from the tail to the head."
history """
    A palmer, the old British fly that Walton already knew, which became a bass fly in America.
    Don Martinez, who tied flies in West Yellowstone in the summers from about 1932 to 1943,
    told Ray Bergman (in Trout) that his Woolly Worms were not his own idea but came from a very
    old Missouri bass fly; he was only the first to sell them as a trout fly. Nobody is recorded
    as the inventor of the bass fly. Wikipedia, citing Gary Soucie, and Orvis put Martinez's
    Woolly Worm in the 1950s, later than his West Yellowstone years. The best-known dressing,
    which George Grant takes for the original, is a thick black chenille body palmered with
    grizzly; he writes that the short red tag was always counted part of it, though modern
    tyers often leave it out. In 1967 Russell Blessing gave it a marabou tail, and it became the Woolly
    Bugger.
    """
fishing """
    A big general nymph or caterpillar. Steve Hudson describes it as an all-purpose nymph:
    weighted to get down near the bottom, under an indicator, or as a dropper below a dry fly or
    a popper. Soft hackle gives it the most life in the water. Smaller ones also pass for
    emergers.
    """
sources {
    "Wikipedia: Woolly Worm" url="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woolly_Worm_(imitation)"
    "Wikipedia: Don Martinez" url="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Martinez"
    "Fly Fishing the Sierra: Woolly Worm" url="https://flyfishingthesierra.com/woolly-worm/"
    "Orvis News: How to Tie the Woolly Worm (Phil Monahan, Tim Flagler)" url="https://news.orvis.com/fly-fishing/video-how-to-tie-the-woolly-worm"
    "Fly Life: Beginner Fly Tying #10, the Woolly Worm (Steve Hudson)" url="https://flylifemagazine.com/beginner-fly-tying-10-the-woolly-worm/"
    "Santa Cruz Fly Fishing: Woolly Worm (Elaine Cook)" url="https://www.santacruzflyfishing.org/2026/05/woolly-worm/"
    "The American Fly Fisher: Don Martinez, Western Dry Fly Master (George Grant, 1982)" url="https://web.archive.org/web/20141129021150/http://www.amff.com/assets/images/archived-journals/1982-Vol09-No2web.pdf"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/YelllowAndGrizzlyWoollyWorm.jpg" page="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YelllowAndGrizzlyWoollyWorm.jpg" author="Mike Cline" license="Public domain" note="Yellow body, red tag, side view in the vise"
    "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/GrizzlyAndOliveWoollyWorm.jpg" page="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GrizzlyAndOliveWoollyWorm.jpg" author="Mike Cline" license="Public domain" note="Olive body, grizzly tail, side view"
    "https://flyfishingthesierra.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/wworm6.jpg" page="https://flyfishingthesierra.com/woolly-worm/" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Black, Martinez style, side view"
}

// Assumption: tied on 3X-long hooks; the 2X-long nymph hook matches the photos' proportions
// better than the 4X-long streamer hook.
hook nymph-2xl size=8
thread black size="6/0"

materials {
    tail wool color=red label="Red wool"
    body chenille color=black
    // Fly Life: fibres one to one and a half gapes; Santa Cruz: a little longer than the gape.
    hackle saddle-hackle color=grizzly fibre=1.2 sweep=25
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.9
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    // Sierra: about half a gape past the bend; Fly Life: about a gape.
    tie-in tail length=0.7 spread=50 note="A short tuft, on top; bind the yarn along the shank for an even underbody."
    tie-in body note="By its stripped core."
    tie-in hackle note="At the bend, by the butt, dull side forward (Martinez)."
    wrap-thread to=0.9
    wind body to=0.9 turns=14 note="Touching turns, an eye-length behind the eye."
    tie-off body
    wind hackle to=0.9 turns=6 note="Even open turns forward over the chenille."
    tie-off hackle
    whip-finish
}
